DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     These functions are designed to simplify access to filesystem quotas.  If
     quotas are active on a filesystem, these functions will access them di‐
     rectly from the kernel using the qquuoottaaccttll() system call.  If quotas are
     not active, these functions will access them by reading and writing the
     quota files directly.

     The qquuoottaa__ooppeenn() function takes a pointer to an _f_s_t_a_b entry corresponding
     to the filesystem on which quotas are to be accessed.  The _q_u_o_t_a_t_y_p_e
     field indicates the type of quotas being sought, either USRQUOTA or
     GRPQUOTA.  The _o_p_e_n_f_l_a_g_s are those used by the ooppeenn() system call, usu‐
     ally either O_RDONLY if the quotas are just to be read, or O_RDWR if the
     quotas are to be updated.  The O_CREAT flag should be specified if a new
     quota file of the requested type should be created if it does not already
     exist.

     The qquuoottaa__cclloossee() function closes any open file descriptors and frees any
     storage associated with the filesystem and quota type referenced by _q_f.

     The qquuoottaa__oonn() function enables quotas for the filesystem associated with
     its _q_f argument which may have been opened with O_RDONLY or O_RDWR.  The
     qquuoottaa__oonn() function returns 0 if successful; otherwise the value -1 is
     returned and the global variable _e_r_r_n_o is set to indicate the error, see
     quotactl(2) for the possible errors.

     The qquuoottaa__ooffff() function disables quotas for the filesystem associated
     with its _q_f argument which may have been opened with O_RDONLY or O_RDWR.
     The qquuoottaa__ooffff() function returns 0 if successful; otherwise the value -1
     is returned and the global variable _e_r_r_n_o is set to indicate the error,
     see quotactl(2) for the possible errors.

     The qquuoottaa__rreeaadd() function reads the quota from the filesystem and quota
     type referenced by _q_f for the user (or group) specified by _i_d into the
     _d_q_b_l_k quota structure pointed to by _d_q_b.

     The qquuoottaa__wwrriittee__lliimmiittss() function updates the limit fields (but not the
     usage fields) for the filesystem and quota type referenced by _q_f for the
     user (or group) specified by _i_d from the _d_q_b_l_k quota structure pointed to
     by _d_q_b.

     The qquuoottaa__wwrriittee__uussaaggee() function updates the usage fields (but not the
     limit fields) for the filesystem and quota type referenced by _q_f for the
     user (or group) specified by _i_d from the _d_q_b_l_k quota structure pointed to
     by _d_q_b.

     The qquuoottaa__ffssnnaammee() function returns a pointer to a buffer containing the
     path to the root of the file system that corresponds to its _q_f argument,
     as listed in _/_e_t_c_/_f_s_t_a_b.  Note that this may be a symbolic link to the
     actual directory.

     The qquuoottaa__qqffnnaammee() function returns a pointer to a buffer containing the
     name of the quota file that corresponds to its _q_f argument.  Note that
     this may be a symbolic link to the actual file.

     The qquuoottaa__mmaaxxiidd() function returns the maximum user (or group) _i_d con‐
     tained in the quota file associated with its _q_f argument.

     The qquuoottaa__cchheecckk__ppaatthh() function checks if the specified path is within
     the filesystem that corresponds to its _q_f argument.  If the _p_a_t_h argument
     refers to a symbolic link, qquuoottaa__cchheecckk__ppaatthh() will follow it.

     The qquuoottaa__ccoonnvveerrtt() function converts the quota file associated with its
     _q_f argument to the data size specified by its _w_o_r_d_s_i_z_e argument.  The
     supported wordsize arguments are 32 for the old 32-bit quota file format
     and 64 for the new 64-bit quota file format.  The qquuoottaa__ccoonnvveerrtt() func‐
     tion may only be called to operate on quota files that are not currently
     active.

IIMMPPLLEEMMEENNTTAATTIIOONN NNOOTTEESS
     If the underlying quota file is in or converted to the old 32-bit format,
     limit and usage values written to the quota file will be clipped to 32
     bits.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     If the filesystem has quotas associated with it, qquuoottaa__ooppeenn() returns a
     pointer to a _q_u_o_t_a_f_i_l_e structure used in subsequent quota access calls.
     If the filesystem has no quotas, or access permission is denied NULL is
     returned and _e_r_r_n_o is set to indicate the error.

     The qquuoottaa__cchheecckk__ppaatthh() function returns 1 for a positive result and 0 for
     a negative result.  If an error occurs, it returns -1 and sets _e_r_r_n_o to
     indicate the error.

     The qquuoottaa__rreeaadd(), qquuoottaa__wwrriittee__lliimmiittss(), qquuoottaa__wwrriittee__uussaaggee(),
     qquuoottaa__ccoonnvveerrtt(), and qquuoottaa__cclloossee() functions return zero on success.  On
     error they return -1 and set _e_r_r_n_o to indicate the error.

